# Sample experiments setup

This folder contains scripts that allow to run the monitor on sets of traces.
By default, some random traces are generated. Use them for testing the setup or for real experiments if that's what
you want. Later in this README, we describe how to customize the scripts here.
The structure of this directory is the following:

```
CMakeLists.txt        CMake configuration.
generate_traces.py    A script to generate random traces.
random-traces         The directory with random traces.
README.md             This README.
run.py                A script that runs the experiments.
```

## Customizing

### `generate_traces.py`

If you have your traces, ignore this script and simply feed them to the monitor
(or the `run.py` script, see below).

If you want to analyze random traces generated in a specific way, you can modify
the `generate_traces.py` script.
By default, this script generates traces with random numbers for the data fields in the events,
where the numbers are constrained as specified in the specification of the monitor.
You can modify the functions `generate_event` and/or `generate_trace` in the script
to generate events as you like.

### `run.py`

This command will run the experiments with pre-defined parameters (the parameters are pre-defined to run short
demo experiments):

```sh
python3 ./run.py
```

See `--help`.

TBD

#### Runs isolation and interference

*The runs are not isolated from other processes in any way.*
As such, they may interfere with each other (if running in parallel), or with other processes on the system.
When measuring time or memory consumption, this might be a problem. You can go around this to some extent by running
the experiments multiple times (e.g., the option `--trials`) and using statistics.
However, in cases where this really matters, you might want to run the experiments in an isolated environment instead of
using the `run.py` script. For that, check, e.g., [BenchExec](https://github.com/sosy-lab/benchexec).

### Running the monitor manually

Running the monitor on the random traces:

```sh
../monitor random-traces
```

Running the monitor on another traces:

```sh
../monitor <traces-dir>
../monitor <trace-1> <trace-2> ...
../monitor <traces-dir-1> <traces-dir-2> <trace-1> <trace-2> ...
```

